== A. Theorem 1(b): v2(sigma(p^e)) = v2(p+1) + v2(e+1) - 1 for e odd, 0 for e even ==
   odd primes p < 200, exponents 1 <= e <= 15: 675 cases, 0 mismatches
   p=  3 (p mod 8 = 3), e= 3: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 3, formula 3
   p=  3 (p mod 8 = 3), e= 5: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 2, formula 2
   p=  3 (p mod 8 = 3), e= 7: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 4, formula 4
   p=  5 (p mod 8 = 5), e= 3: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 2, formula 2
   p=  5 (p mod 8 = 5), e= 5: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 1, formula 1
   p=  5 (p mod 8 = 5), e= 7: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 3, formula 3
   p=  7 (p mod 8 = 7), e= 7: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 5, formula 5
   p= 13 (p mod 8 = 5), e= 3: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 2, formula 2
   p= 31 (p mod 8 = 7), e= 7: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 7, formula 7
   p= 17 (p mod 8 = 1), e=15: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 4, formula 4
   p=  3 (p mod 8 = 3), e=15: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 5, formula 5
   p=199 (p mod 8 = 7), e= 9: v2(sigma(p^e)) = 3, formula 3
   v2(sigma(m)) = sum over odd-exponent p^e || m of (v2(p+1)+v2(e+1)-1), all odd m < 10^6: 0 mismatches
   remark on the note's PROOF of (b): '1 + p^2 + ... + p^(e-1) is a sum of (e+1)/2 odd terms, so its v2 is v2((e+1)/2)'
   is not a valid inference by itself (1 + 3 = 4 is a sum of 2 odd terms with v2 = 2 != v2(2) = 1); it holds here because
   the terms are = 1 mod 8 (pins v2 when (e+1)/2 is not divisible by 8) and in general by LTE:
   1 + p^2 + ... + p^(2k-2) = (p^(2k) - 1)/(p^2 - 1), v2 = [v2(p-1)+v2(p+1)+v2(2k)-1] - [v2(p-1)+v2(p+1)] = v2(k).
   example needing LTE: p=3, e=15: 1+9+...+9^7 = 5380840 = 8 * 672605, v2 = 3 = v2(8) (8 | (e+1)/2, so 'mod 8' gives only >= 3)
[  23.4s]

